              "message": "Store: each call writes its own entries and the objects it changed\n\nThe coordinators wrote the whole workspace, its log included, as one JSON\ndocument after every call, so the cost of a call grew with the log and a\nlong run slowed with the square of its length.\n\n- Both coordinators record which objects a call touched, and write once\n  when the call ends, in one transaction: the call's audit entries, those\n  objects and the workspace's own fields, through a new, optional\n  Store.apply. A store with only save is sent the whole workspace, as\n  before.\n- SqliteStore keeps three tables: chap_meta, chap_entries and\n  chap_objects. Opening a file from an earlier release moves its\n  workspaces into them, in the transaction that puts a view named\n  chap_workspaces in the old table's place, so scripts that read the old\n  table read the current state; the old table is kept as\n  chap_workspaces_v1. Both coordinators write the same tables and the same\n  entries, and read a file in one transaction.\n- Each write names the audit entries it expects the store to hold, and\n  the shipped stores refuse a write that does not match with\n  StoreConflictError. A second writer is reported, and the entries stored\n  are kept. SPECIFICATION 10.3, the Handbook and SECURITY.md say so.\n- A failed write is reported on stderr, or on the chap_coordinator logger,\n  when no handler is set, and the next write carries what it did not.\n  Python gains on_store_error, and its store recovers from a failed\n  commit.\n- A handler works on its own copy of a call's params, and the caller gets\n  a copy of the result, so nothing the caller holds is shared with the\n  workspace. A TypeScript reviewer list reused for two review requests was\n  shared by both tasks.\n- A workspace that cannot be restored is named in a warning, and the\n  others load. SqliteStore loads from CommonJS.\n- The templates' analytics.py and the reference analyze_overrides.py read\n  the new tables and an earlier file; the starter writes through apply.\n- Tests in both languages cover a flat write size, a restart across every\n  collection, a lapsed whisper, failed and async writes, two writers, the\n  upgrade from an earlier file, an application's own view of the old\n  table, unreadable rows and shared objects. The differential fuzzer also\n  replays each seed into both coordinators on SQLite files, and checks\n  each restart and that the two files hold the same entries.\n\nIssue-flagged-by: Alan Ryan <alan.ryan@brightbeam.com>",
