          "context": "## What was asked\n\nAlan found that `SqliteStore.save` rewrote the whole workspace, its log\nincluded, as one JSON document after every call, so a run's cost grew with\nthe square of its length, and he had to fall back to memory. Fix it for\n0.3.2, in both coordinators, so the store matches what CHAP describes: an\nappend-only log.\n\n## What changed, and why\n\n- **Each call writes what it changed, once.** The coordinators record which\n  objects a call touched, and when the call ends they write, in one\n  transaction, the call's audit entries, those objects and the workspace's\n  own fields. This goes through a new, optional `Store.apply`; a store with\n  only `save` is sent the whole workspace, as before.\n- **Three tables.** `chap_meta`, `chap_entries` (one row per audit entry)\n  and `chap_objects` (one row per member, task, override and so on). A file\n  from 0.3.1 or earlier is moved into them when it is opened, and the old\n  table is kept as `chap_workspaces_v1`. A view named `chap_workspaces`\n  takes its place, so analytics scripts in projects generated earlier keep\n  working and read the current state. A 0.3.1 coordinator refuses to open\n  the upgraded file.\n- **A second writer is refused.** Each write names how many entries the\n  store should already hold. A mismatch is refused and reported, and nothing\n  stored is overwritten. Before, the stale writer silently replaced the\n  other's entries. SPECIFICATION 10.3, the Handbook and SECURITY.md describe\n  both cases.\n- **Failures are loud.** A failed write is printed or logged when no handler\n  is set, and the next write carries what it missed. Python gains\n  `on_store_error`.\n- **Found on the way, and fixed.** A call's params and result are now\n  copied, so nothing a caller holds is shared with the workspace (a reviewer\n  list reused for two review requests in TypeScript was shared by both\n  tasks). `SqliteStore` now loads when required from CommonJS. A workspace\n  that cannot be restored is named in a warning.\n- **Readers.** `chap-analytics` 0.2.3, the templates' `analytics.py` and\n  the reference `analyze_overrides.py` read the new tables and an older\n  file, each in one read transaction.\n- **The release.** Every protocol package moves to 0.3.2;\n  `chap-analytics` to 0.2.3. The changelog has the entry.\n\n## How it was tested\n\n- Measured: in Python, 2,000 calls took about a minute with 0.3.1 and about\n  a second now; in TypeScript, the time per call stays flat as the log\n  grows, where 0.3.1's grew with it.\n- New tests in both languages: write size stays flat over a long run; a\n  restart reproduces every collection; a whisper that lapses outside a call\n  is written; failed and async writes are carried by the next; two writers;\n  the upgrade from an older file, including an application's own view of\n  the old table; unreadable rows; shared objects.\n- The differential fuzzer now also replays each seed into both coordinators\n  on SQLite files, checks that a restart reproduces the state, and that the\n  two files hold the same audit entries.\n- Every test suite, the conformance harness against both references, every\n  template generated and tested, the version, schema, catalogue and docs\n  checks.\n- An independent review of the change found no blockers; its findings are\n  fixed and have tests.\n\n## Look closely at\n\n- The first commit, `Store: each call writes its own entries and the\n  objects it changed`: `packages/coordinator/src/coordinator.ts` (change\n  tracking and writes), the two `storage/sqlite` files (tables, upgrade,\n  view) and `tracked.ts`/`tracked.py`.\n- SPECIFICATION 10.3, for the wording on a second writer.\n- The changelog entry, for what users are told about the upgraded file."
